WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.310
- You have questions?

00:00:01.310 --> 00:00:02.900
I sometimes have answers.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.660
Bear's ready. I'm ready. Are you ready?

00:00:05.162 --> 00:00:07.342
Robin, Discord, "Why do
people twitch in the middle

00:00:07.342 --> 00:00:09.532
of their sleep and scare
the peewoop outta me?"

00:00:09.532 --> 00:00:11.032
You go through different stages of sleep,

00:00:11.032 --> 00:00:12.552
and when you're in your
deepest stage of sleep,

00:00:12.552 --> 00:00:15.792
you actually turn off your
musculoskeletal movements.

00:00:15.792 --> 00:00:17.522
That neurological system shuts down

00:00:17.522 --> 00:00:19.342
but sometimes when you're
going in between stages,

00:00:19.342 --> 00:00:21.462
it doesn't fully shut down
and you're still in sleep

00:00:21.462 --> 00:00:23.102
and you're dreaming so
some of those movements,

00:00:23.102 --> 00:00:24.352
actually do get through.

00:00:24.352 --> 00:00:26.832
Bear once did that too and kick me.

00:00:26.832 --> 00:00:27.939
I was upset with him.

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:30.972
"What actually causes the
sensation of an itch?"

00:00:30.972 --> 00:00:32.642
Well, this is a complex process

00:00:32.642 --> 00:00:34.362
that's primarily histamine driven.

00:00:34.362 --> 00:00:37.622
There is a specific type
of nerve cell fiber known

00:00:37.622 --> 00:00:38.682
as the C fiber.

00:00:38.682 --> 00:00:41.652
These are the receptors
that actually initiate

00:00:41.652 --> 00:00:42.792
the sensation of an itch.

00:00:42.792 --> 00:00:46.702
Usually, a response to a
mechanical or chemical process.

00:00:46.702 --> 00:00:47.992
It's an inflammatory response.

00:00:47.992 --> 00:00:50.442
It's a form of an inflammatory response.

00:00:50.442 --> 00:00:52.009
Discord, Naomi multifandom,

00:00:52.009 --> 00:00:54.912
"Why are vaccines usually
administered in the shoulder?"

00:00:54.912 --> 00:00:57.152
Well, it's a great deltoid muscle.

00:00:57.152 --> 00:00:58.142
A lot of circulation.

00:00:58.142 --> 00:00:59.012
It's quick. It's easy.

00:00:59.012 --> 00:01:00.842
You don't have to get
really fully undressed

00:01:00.842 --> 00:01:02.292
in order to make that injection happen.

00:01:02.292 --> 00:01:04.602
Back in the day we used
to use the gluteus maximus

00:01:04.602 --> 00:01:05.502
or the buttock.

00:01:05.502 --> 00:01:07.972
Now, it's a little bit invasive
and it's easier to just go

00:01:07.972 --> 00:01:09.512
in the shoulder and do the same thing.

00:01:09.512 --> 00:01:10.839
YouTube, The House Office,

00:01:10.839 --> 00:01:14.692
"Why are you a fluffy
cinnamon roll I must protect?"

00:01:14.692 --> 00:01:16.812
First of all, I ain't
no cinnamon roll, okay?

00:01:16.812 --> 00:01:18.272
The reason I aint no cinnamon roll

00:01:18.272 --> 00:01:20.042
is because while I am delicious,

00:01:20.042 --> 00:01:21.035
I am healthy.

00:01:23.432 --> 00:01:25.402
Like a doctor?

00:01:25.402 --> 00:01:26.905
- [Sam] I even sensed it's coming--

00:01:26.905 --> 00:01:28.682
- But you don't know what
direction they're coming from.

00:01:28.682 --> 00:01:29.515
- [Sam] There is no preparing--

00:01:29.515 --> 00:01:30.687
- You don't know what
the word is gonna be.

00:01:30.687 --> 00:01:32.959
- [Sam] You can't get ready
for something like that.

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:35.302
- "What can cause a constant
ringing in someone's ears

00:01:35.302 --> 00:01:37.432
other than loud music noise,
what can get rid of it?"

00:01:37.432 --> 00:01:39.432
This is really interesting
because tinnitus

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:42.022
is a constant ringing in the
ears and it can be a ringing,

00:01:42.022 --> 00:01:42.855
could be a buzzing,

00:01:42.855 --> 00:01:44.232
it's all sorts of sounds that can happen.

00:01:44.232 --> 00:01:46.772
And a lot of times, the
reason why my patients come in

00:01:46.772 --> 00:01:50.482
is as a result of cerumen
impaction or ear wax blockages.

00:01:50.482 --> 00:01:53.482
Another thing that causes it
is sensorineural hearing loss.

00:01:53.482 --> 00:01:55.612
You actually develop hearing loss

00:01:55.612 --> 00:01:57.182
for one of many different reasons

00:01:57.182 --> 00:02:00.022
and as a result of losing actual hearing,

00:02:00.022 --> 00:02:02.352
you start hearing ringing in your ears,

00:02:02.352 --> 00:02:04.522
which is manufactured by the actual nerve

00:02:04.522 --> 00:02:05.612
that transmits the sound.

00:02:05.612 --> 00:02:07.562
A lot of people think it means
you're hearing something.

00:02:07.562 --> 00:02:09.722
It actually could be a
form of hearing loss.

00:02:09.722 --> 00:02:12.322
That's why it's very important
to always get it checked out.

00:02:12.322 --> 00:02:14.802
Discord, Arielle, "A lot
of medical shows, movies,

00:02:14.802 --> 00:02:17.202
you'll see med students
shocked at being exposed

00:02:17.202 --> 00:02:18.562
to cadavers for the first time.

00:02:18.562 --> 00:02:19.822
Does this ever happen in real life,

00:02:19.822 --> 00:02:21.442
where seeing a dead
person for the first time,

00:02:21.442 --> 00:02:22.892
a med student will get sick or faint?"

00:02:22.892 --> 00:02:23.872
Yeah, it happens.

00:02:23.872 --> 00:02:27.312
Also, the fumes of the
formaldehyde are pretty stenchy,

00:02:27.312 --> 00:02:28.172
if that's a word.

00:02:28.172 --> 00:02:30.422
I was kind of in awe the
first time that I saw them,

00:02:30.422 --> 00:02:32.572
but the scary part was when I was studying

00:02:32.572 --> 00:02:34.582
for an exam late Friday night,

00:02:34.582 --> 00:02:35.932
that was for an exam on Monday,

00:02:35.932 --> 00:02:37.512
being alone in a cadaver lab,

00:02:37.512 --> 00:02:38.432
in the dark,
(thunder rumbling)

00:02:38.432 --> 00:02:41.852
hearing noises, thunderstorm
outside, no-brainer.

00:02:41.852 --> 00:02:43.829
Discord, Jess Michiyo,

00:02:43.829 --> 00:02:46.232
"I always get a runny nose
because of spicy food.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:47.152
What is that about?"

00:02:47.152 --> 00:02:48.702
That is about capsaicin.

00:02:48.702 --> 00:02:52.432
It is a nutrient that
is found in spicy foods

00:02:52.432 --> 00:02:56.162
that actually bind to specific
receptors in your body,

00:02:56.162 --> 00:02:57.902
giving you the sensation of burning.

00:02:57.902 --> 00:03:00.052
There's no actual burning happening.

00:03:00.052 --> 00:03:01.842
It's tricking your body
to think it's on fire

00:03:01.842 --> 00:03:03.912
and as a result, your body protects itself

00:03:03.912 --> 00:03:06.712
by trying to lubricate the area,
releasing mucus, et cetera.

00:03:06.712 --> 00:03:08.589
It's a great way to clear
your sinuses, by the way.

00:03:08.589 --> 00:03:11.472
"I'm considering buying
a posture corrector,

00:03:11.472 --> 00:03:12.712
do they really work?"

00:03:12.712 --> 00:03:15.162
Generally speaking here, I would say, no.

00:03:15.162 --> 00:03:18.132
I want you, when you come
in with a poor posture

00:03:18.132 --> 00:03:20.592
is to figure out A, what's causing it,

00:03:20.592 --> 00:03:23.472
and B, to actually allow
your body to fix it.

00:03:23.472 --> 00:03:27.252
Or if it's bad enough posture
for an anatomical concern,

00:03:27.252 --> 00:03:29.572
refer you to an orthopaedic
specialist to figure out

00:03:29.572 --> 00:03:30.405
why that's happening.

00:03:30.405 --> 00:03:33.502
Posture correctors almost end
up doing more harm than good

00:03:33.502 --> 00:03:35.942
in many cases, where they
weaken your core muscles,

00:03:35.942 --> 00:03:37.742
your stability muscles, and as a result,

00:03:37.742 --> 00:03:39.412
making you more likely to get injured.

00:03:39.412 --> 00:03:41.912
There are certain ones on the
market for specific conditions

00:03:41.912 --> 00:03:44.402
that may work but I would
generally try and stay away

00:03:44.402 --> 00:03:46.762
unless your doctor
absolutely recommends them.

00:03:46.762 --> 00:03:49.312
Discord, Kristen g, what up Discord?

00:03:49.312 --> 00:03:50.145
For those of you who don't know,

00:03:50.145 --> 00:03:51.902
we have a private Discord where we chat.

00:03:51.902 --> 00:03:53.829
Patreon members only, sign up down below.

00:03:53.829 --> 00:03:55.552
"What's the best way to
get electrolytes after

00:03:55.552 --> 00:03:57.252
a hard workout without adding sugar?"

00:03:57.252 --> 00:03:59.572
Electrolytes are not added through sugar.

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.622
Electrolytes are essentially salts

00:04:01.622 --> 00:04:03.182
that your body needs to function.

00:04:03.182 --> 00:04:04.552
And the way that you get these

00:04:04.552 --> 00:04:06.962
is by eating foods or beverages

00:04:06.962 --> 00:04:08.372
that contain those electrolytes.

00:04:08.372 --> 00:04:10.172
Sodium doesn't taste great after a workout

00:04:10.172 --> 00:04:11.652
but after a grueling one,

00:04:11.652 --> 00:04:13.292
it really does help you absorb the water

00:04:13.292 --> 00:04:14.212
and stay more hydrated.

00:04:14.212 --> 00:04:15.842
Back in the day they
used to use pickle juice.

00:04:15.842 --> 00:04:17.032
Sure, tastes good too.

00:04:17.032 --> 00:04:18.322
Not bad, I like it.

00:04:18.322 --> 00:04:19.572
I like pickles (mumbles).

00:04:21.866 --> 00:04:24.339
"Is there a way for a civilian
to test and see what kind

00:04:24.339 --> 00:04:26.836
of nutrients their body
has a lot or a little of?

00:04:26.836 --> 00:04:28.549
I'm always confused about what to eat

00:04:28.549 --> 00:04:30.209
and it would be great to
know if I'm eating too much

00:04:30.209 --> 00:04:31.069
or too little of something."

00:04:31.069 --> 00:04:32.899
Currently, there's no
such thing on the market.

00:04:32.899 --> 00:04:35.649
Yes, we can do specific blood
tests to see if you're low

00:04:35.649 --> 00:04:37.829
on certain vitamins or if you're anemic

00:04:37.829 --> 00:04:38.662
and things like that.

00:04:38.662 --> 00:04:40.329
But generally we don't
screen for these things,

00:04:40.329 --> 00:04:42.479
if you feel great and
you have no symptoms,

00:04:42.479 --> 00:04:43.959
there's no point looking for them.

00:04:43.959 --> 00:04:45.909
'Cause a lot of times, if you feel good

00:04:45.909 --> 00:04:47.439
and your lab values a little bit off,

00:04:47.439 --> 00:04:49.499
we still decide not to the treat the value

00:04:49.499 --> 00:04:51.419
because the reality is
we treat the patient,

00:04:51.419 --> 00:04:52.456
not the lab values.

00:04:52.456 --> 00:04:54.239
"Why do our stomachs growl?"

00:04:54.239 --> 00:04:55.869
Usually 'cause they're angry.

00:04:55.869 --> 00:04:56.702
No, I'm kidding.

00:04:56.702 --> 00:04:59.199
The reason they growl is
because of peristalsis.

00:04:59.199 --> 00:05:02.639
The muscles in the stomach,
actually try and churn the food

00:05:02.639 --> 00:05:03.609
in order to be...

00:05:03.609 --> 00:05:06.059
Act as a form of mechanical digestion.

00:05:06.059 --> 00:05:07.799
'Cause remember, there's
multiple types of digestion,

00:05:07.799 --> 00:05:10.669
there's chemical, mechanical,
mechanical being chewing,

00:05:10.669 --> 00:05:12.739
peristalsis, the stomach churning,

00:05:12.739 --> 00:05:14.159
and that makes a growling noise.

00:05:14.159 --> 00:05:16.949
Also, remember when
you're dealing with fluids

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:18.619
and there's bacteria inside our gut,

00:05:18.619 --> 00:05:19.929
there's also gases released.

00:05:19.929 --> 00:05:22.929
That gas, as it makes its way
through the digestive system,

00:05:22.929 --> 00:05:24.039
can make those noises

00:05:24.039 --> 00:05:25.629
and then at the end of the day, we fart.

00:05:25.629 --> 00:05:26.836
Lance McPhee, YouTube,

00:05:26.836 --> 00:05:28.999
"How often should you
shower and wash your hair?"

00:05:28.999 --> 00:05:31.749
I have this argument so often

00:05:31.749 --> 00:05:34.529
because there are people
who say you have to shower

00:05:34.529 --> 00:05:36.849
every single day and
should washed your hair

00:05:36.849 --> 00:05:37.729
every single day.

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:39.699
And that is in fact not reality.

00:05:39.699 --> 00:05:41.239
The decision to wash your hair

00:05:41.239 --> 00:05:44.179
should really be individualized
one, a holistic one.

00:05:44.179 --> 00:05:46.429
Meaning that yes, if
your hair is very greasy,

00:05:46.429 --> 00:05:48.949
or your scalp is itchy, or
there's a lot of dandruff,

00:05:48.949 --> 00:05:50.479
maybe a good reason to wash your hair.

00:05:50.479 --> 00:05:51.879
Yes, if you come home and you're smelly

00:05:51.879 --> 00:05:53.119
from the gym, please shower.

00:05:53.119 --> 00:05:55.269
But otherwise, like if
you just had a normal day,

00:05:55.269 --> 00:05:57.269
it's not absolutely
mandatory that you shower,

00:05:57.269 --> 00:05:58.869
and it's not absolutely
mandatory that you need

00:05:58.869 --> 00:05:59.702
to use shampoo.

00:05:59.702 --> 00:06:01.049
And depending on your hair type,

00:06:01.049 --> 00:06:03.109
also decides how often you
should be washing your hair.

00:06:03.109 --> 00:06:05.979
Those with very fine hair, maybe
every day, every other day.

00:06:05.979 --> 00:06:08.399
Those with more textured
hair can go several days

00:06:08.399 --> 00:06:09.339
without washing their hair

00:06:09.339 --> 00:06:11.519
but know that the more
often you wash your hair,

00:06:11.519 --> 00:06:13.069
the more that you actually take off

00:06:13.069 --> 00:06:15.359
some of those important
oils off of your scalp,

00:06:15.359 --> 00:06:17.469
you can actually cause
damage, more irritation,

00:06:17.469 --> 00:06:19.129
dry out your hair, et cetera, et cetera.

00:06:19.129 --> 00:06:20.799
When it comes to showering, same thing,

00:06:20.799 --> 00:06:22.239
your skin doesn't like hot water,

00:06:22.239 --> 00:06:24.979
your skin doesn't like having
soap all over it all the time.

00:06:24.979 --> 00:06:27.459
In fact, your shower should
be really, ideally short,

00:06:27.459 --> 00:06:29.249
like three to four
minutes, just focusing on

00:06:29.249 --> 00:06:30.559
like you're growing your armpits.

00:06:30.559 --> 00:06:31.606
That's about it.

00:06:31.606 --> 00:06:35.179
"Why do so many people set a
step goal count of 10K a day?

00:06:35.179 --> 00:06:37.229
Is there any scientific reason for this?"

00:06:38.532 --> 00:06:39.459
(shouts loudly)

00:06:39.459 --> 00:06:41.439
The 10K thing doesn't really mean much.

00:06:41.439 --> 00:06:45.089
Look, whatever way you
choose to get active,

00:06:45.089 --> 00:06:46.159
it will be healthy for you.

00:06:46.159 --> 00:06:49.689
If you're increasing from 5K
steps to 7K steps, amazing.

00:06:49.689 --> 00:06:51.219
If you've been working out one day a week

00:06:51.219 --> 00:06:53.289
and you've gone to three
days a week, amazing.

00:06:53.289 --> 00:06:55.301
I will never be that
rigid doctor that's like,

00:06:55.301 --> 00:06:56.416
(sighs deeply)

00:06:56.416 --> 00:07:01.416
"I see by your watch here,
you've averaged 9999.9 steps.

00:07:02.429 --> 00:07:05.786
Disappointed, it's time for
me to cut you as a patient."

00:07:05.786 --> 00:07:07.359
"Is eating burned food bad for you?"

00:07:07.359 --> 00:07:09.379
Actually it is, NekoMouser,

00:07:09.379 --> 00:07:12.319
you actually create all these
cancer causing chemicals

00:07:12.319 --> 00:07:14.899
when you eat burned foods, so
ideally you wanna stay away

00:07:14.899 --> 00:07:17.159
from charred as much as possible.

00:07:17.159 --> 00:07:20.159
In fact, marinating
your meats help prevent

00:07:20.159 --> 00:07:21.549
some of those hydrocarbons

00:07:21.549 --> 00:07:23.309
and other toxic nutrients from forming.

00:07:23.309 --> 00:07:25.179
Not a nutrients, chemicals from forming.

00:07:25.179 --> 00:07:27.679
Actually, eating greens with
your meats helps decrease

00:07:27.679 --> 00:07:29.479
some of the cancer
causing minerals as well.

00:07:29.479 --> 00:07:30.549
Keep calling them different things,

00:07:30.549 --> 00:07:32.959
nutrients, minerals, they're chemicals.

00:07:32.959 --> 00:07:34.486
Chewy time.

00:07:34.486 --> 00:07:35.729
"Does banging one's head

00:07:35.729 --> 00:07:38.729
from the side actually shake
water out from your ears

00:07:38.729 --> 00:07:40.359
or is that something they do in cartoons?"

00:07:40.359 --> 00:07:41.269
Don't bang your head.

00:07:41.269 --> 00:07:43.269
That's how concussions happen. Just jump

00:07:44.149 --> 00:07:45.992
and the gravity will do the trick.

00:07:48.924 --> 00:07:50.406
(laughs loudly)

00:07:50.406 --> 00:07:51.829
"If someone gets stabbed in the stomach,

00:07:51.829 --> 00:07:53.799
will the stomach acid
seep out into their body

00:07:53.799 --> 00:07:55.519
